From 14766db68f87564f4ba7f2e49be2c7d65d7c634f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stephan Raj Ignatious Durairaj <signatio@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Thu, 21 May 2026 19:59:22 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] agent/bwl/bpl: set multi_ap_vlanid on backhaul AP for traffic
 separation

In a daisy-chain topology with traffic separation enabled, Agent1 must
include the primary VLAN ID in its backhaul AP association responses so
that Agent2 can learn the VLAN ID when it connects wirelessly.

This patch implements the full pipeline to propagate the primary VLAN ID
received from the controller (via tlvProfile2Default802dotQSettings in
M2) into the running hostapd configuration of the backhaul AP.

Problem 1 — Timing: set_primary_vlan_id() is called during M2 processing
before the backhaul AP is created (the AP is created later in the same
M2 handler). The loop over available_vaps finds no backhaul VAP and
returns without setting multi_ap_vlanid.

Fix: Store the primary VLAN ID in m_primary_vlan_id when
set_primary_vlan_id() is called. create_new_hostapd_conf_file() now
embeds multi_ap_vlanid directly in the backhaul AP conf file at creation
time, so hostapd advertises the VLAN ID in association responses from
the moment the AP comes up — without requiring a subsequent
RELOAD_CONFIG.

Problem 2 — RELOAD_CONFIG target: The backhaul AP conf file is created
as hostapd-<phy>.<base_iface>.conf with "interface=<base_iface>".
Sending RELOAD_CONFIG to the link-specific name (e.g. wlan1_3_link0)
does not reload this per-interface conf file. RELOAD_CONFIG must target
the base interface (e.g. wlan1_3).

Fix: Send RELOAD_CONFIG to base_iface, not vap.second.bss.

Problem 3 — VAP section fallback: When the BSSID lookup in the conf
file fails (link-specific BSSID differs from the conf BSSID), the
fallback used vap.second.bss (link-specific name) which does not match
any VAP section in the conf file. The correct fallback is base_iface.

Fix: Use base_iface as the fallback target_vap.

Problem 4 — Cross-radio MLO: Sending RELOAD_CONFIG to a peer-radio
interface (e.g. wlan0_1 from wlan2's ap_manager) causes hostapd to
reconfigure the entire MLD and removes the control socket for wlan2,
triggering an ap_manager restart loop.

Fix: Detect cross-radio MLO links by checking whether the backhaul BSS
name contains the base name of the current radio. Skip RELOAD_CONFIG for
cross-radio links; the UCI update persists the value for the next reload.

Problem 5 — M1 ordering: When traffic separation is active, M1 must not
be sent until the VLAN subinterface has been created on the backhaul
trunk. Sending M1 too early causes the controller to include MLO
configuration in M2 before the agent is ready.

Fix: Gate M1 on ts_applied (set by TrafficSeparationTask after a
successful full reset). Add ts_applied flag to AgentDB.

Problem 6 — MLD re-autoconfiguration: A radio that received M2 before
the AP MLD configuration request arrived gets mld_id=-1 for all its
BSSes. When the MLD config later arrives, the radio must re-run M1/M2
to obtain the correct MLD IDs.

Fix: In handle_agent_ap_mld_configuration_tlv(), trigger
re-autoconfiguration for radios newly added to MLD configuration while
already in CONFIGURED state.

New BPL API: cfg_set_wifi_iface_multi_ap_vlanid() persists
multi_ap_vlanid in both wifi-iface and wifi-mld UCI sections for the
given interface name. Implemented for UCI; stub for linux/dm/rdk.

Signed-off-by: Stephan Raj Ignatious Durairaj <signatio@qti.qualcomm.com>
